WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:04.521
GraphiQL is the in-browser editor of the GraphQL module.

00:00:04.521 --> 00:00:08.553
You write a query, run it against your site, and read the result.

00:00:08.553 --> 00:00:12.528
In this video, we'll build and run queries, and see how the page

00:00:12.528 --> 00:00:15.140
talks to the endpoint your clients call.

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:24.009
GraphiQL is a page of the admin, and it is part of the GraphQL feature.

00:00:24.009 --> 00:00:28.499
It sends your queries to the same endpoint that your applications call, and

00:00:28.499 --> 00:00:31.160
it runs them as the user who is signed in.

00:00:31.910 --> 00:00:33.925
Each run is one request.

00:00:33.925 --> 00:00:40.371
GraphiQL posts a JSON body with the query, and optionally variables and an operation name.

00:00:40.371 --> 00:00:44.870
The response is JSON too, and it holds data, errors, or both.

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:52.434
In the admin menu, open Tools, then GraphiQL.

00:00:52.434 --> 00:00:57.106
The item is there when the GraphQL feature is enabled and your user

00:00:57.106 --> 00:00:59.730
has the Execute GraphQL permission.

00:01:00.203 --> 00:01:01.906
The screen has three areas.

00:01:01.906 --> 00:01:05.519
The Explorer on the left lists what you can query, the editor in

00:01:05.519 --> 00:01:09.133
the middle holds the query, and the result appears on the right.

00:01:09.631 --> 00:01:11.934
The editor starts with comments.

00:01:11.934 --> 00:01:16.540
Lines that begin with a hash are ignored, and they list the shortcuts:

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:20.601
Ctrl-Enter runs the query, and Ctrl-Space opens autocomplete.

00:01:21.067 --> 00:01:25.747
Above the editor, the play button runs the query, and Prettify formats it.

00:01:25.747 --> 00:01:30.428
History lists the queries you ran before, and the Explorer button shows or

00:01:30.428 --> 00:01:32.417
hides the panel on the left.

00:01:36.694 --> 00:01:39.026
Let's build a query without typing.

00:01:39.026 --> 00:01:41.188
In the Explorer, click blogPost.

00:01:41.188 --> 00:01:45.510
Your content types are fields here, such as blog and blogPost, and the

00:01:45.510 --> 00:01:47.444
query appears in the editor.

00:01:47.925 --> 00:01:51.691
Tick displayText and createdUtc so that both are in the query.

00:01:51.691 --> 00:01:55.790
Then tick path, which comes from the Autoroute part of the blog post

00:01:55.790 --> 00:01:59.225
and is listed with the other fields of the content item.

00:01:59.693 --> 00:02:01.340
Press the play button.

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:05.810
The result appears on the right as JSON, with one entry under blogPost

00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:09.633
for each published blog post of the site, the newest first.

00:02:10.118 --> 00:02:13.052
Untick createdUtc, and it leaves the query.

00:02:13.052 --> 00:02:17.242
The Explorer and the editor share the same query, so a change in

00:02:17.242 --> 00:02:18.978
one shows in the other.

00:02:23.225 --> 00:02:27.983
To read the schema itself, hide the Explorer to make room, and click Docs.

00:02:27.983 --> 00:02:32.324
The Documentation Explorer opens with the root types of the schema.

00:02:32.324 --> 00:02:34.525
Click Query to list its fields.

00:02:35.039 --> 00:02:37.654
Search for blogPost, and open the field.

00:02:37.654 --> 00:02:43.339
Its description reads Represents a Blog Post, built from the display name of the content type.

00:02:43.831 --> 00:02:48.180
Below it, the arguments are listed, each with a description:

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:51.211
first, orderBy, skip, status, and where.

00:02:51.211 --> 00:02:55.231
A separate video covers filtering and paging with them.

00:02:59.431 --> 00:03:05.063
GraphiQL keeps the query, the variables, and the operation name in the address of the page.

00:03:05.063 --> 00:03:09.301
Bookmark the address, or send it to a colleague, and the same query

00:03:09.301 --> 00:03:10.811
opens in the editor.

00:03:12.443 --> 00:03:16.783
Here the page is opened from such an address, so the query arrives

00:03:16.783 --> 00:03:19.375
on one line, and Prettify formats it.

00:03:19.375 --> 00:03:24.378
The query declares a variable named first, and the Query Variables pane gives

00:03:24.378 --> 00:03:25.643
it the value 2.

00:03:26.110 --> 00:03:28.551
Run it, and two blog posts come back.

00:03:28.551 --> 00:03:32.922
Change the value to 1 in the pane and run it again, and one comes back.

00:03:32.922 --> 00:03:35.420
The text of the query does not change.

00:03:39.622 --> 00:03:43.448
Ask for a field that does not exist, such as title on blogPost.

00:03:43.448 --> 00:03:48.051
The editor underlines it before you run anything, because it checks the query

00:03:48.051 --> 00:03:49.992
against the schema it loaded.

00:03:50.455 --> 00:03:55.392
Run it anyway, and the response has an errors list instead of data.

00:03:55.392 --> 00:04:00.395
GraphiQL shows the body of the response whatever the HTTP status is.

00:04:04.595 --> 00:04:07.076
Behind the play button is a fetch call.

00:04:07.076 --> 00:04:10.660
It posts the query as JSON to the endpoint of the site, and

00:04:10.660 --> 00:04:15.345
includes the credentials of your session, so the signed-in user runs the query.

00:04:16.095 --> 00:04:19.712
Your own client sends the same body to the same path.

00:04:19.712 --> 00:04:24.310
It authenticates with a cookie or an OAuth access token, and the user

00:04:24.310 --> 00:04:26.945
needs the Execute GraphQL permission.

00:04:27.695 --> 00:04:30.340
The HTTP status tells the outcome.

00:04:30.340 --> 00:04:35.298
A query without errors returns 200, a failed query returns 400, and a

00:04:35.298 --> 00:04:39.595
query that needs a permission the caller lacks returns 401.

00:04:41.595 --> 00:04:42.841
That's GraphiQL:

00:04:42.841 --> 00:04:48.163
the Explorer builds queries, Docs describes the schema, and the address keeps your work.

00:04:48.163 --> 00:04:49.635
Thanks for watching.

